@[iso-kernel step]
Installing the ISO kernel is easy, and it should work well (it's working on
your computer right now): the kernel, its modules and the firmware they load
(Wi-Fi, GPU, ...) are copied to the new system. The @[grub] step then writes
a boot menu entry for it.

using enchantment:
  @[enchantment install iso-kernel]

manually (what that command does):
  @[cp /boot/config-$(uname -r) /boot/System.map-$(uname -r) $ENCHANT_TARGET/boot/]
  @[cp /boot/kernel $ENCHANT_TARGET/boot/vmlinuz-$(uname -r)]
  @[mkdir -p $ENCHANT_TARGET/lib/modules $ENCHANT_TARGET/lib/firmware]
  @[cp -a /lib/modules/$(uname -r) $ENCHANT_TARGET/lib/modules/]
  @[cp -a /lib/firmware/. $ENCHANT_TARGET/lib/firmware/]
  @[depmod -b $ENCHANT_TARGET $(uname -r)]

If another system's GRUB starts this one through os-prober: os-prober takes
the kernels from this system's /boot/grub/grub.cfg when there is one, so
rerun grub-mkconfig here after adding a kernel (or it won't be offered).

Type @[next] to go on to the boot loader (or @[todo] for the kernel step).
